Honestly, you've already crossed the line from "learning project" into "actually useful installer." If you wanted to make it something other people would use and recommend, I'd focus on flexibility, safety, and quality-of-life features rather than adding a ton of complexity.

Tier 1: Must-Have Features

These are the things I'd add first.

1. BIOS/Legacy Boot Support

Right now the script requires UEFI.

Supporting both:

UEFI
BIOS/Legacy

would greatly expand compatibility.

The installer could automatically detect:

Shell
if [[ -d /sys/firmware/efi ]]; then
BOOT_MODE="uefi"
else
BOOT_MODE="bios"
fi

and install GRUB appropriately.

2. Create a User Account

Right now users only get:

Plain Text
root

Most users expect:

Plain Text
root
jacob

Prompt for:

Username
Password
Whether the user should be a sudoer

Example:

Shell
useradd -m -G wheel jacob
passwd jacob
 

Then uncomment:

Plain Text
%wheel ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL

in sudoers.

3. Better Partition Layout Choices

Currently:

Plain Text
1G EFI
8G Swap
Remaining Root

A menu would be useful:

Plain Text
1. Simple ext4 layout
2. Btrfs layout
3. Custom partitioning

Most users could pick option 1.

4. Better Validation

Validate:

Hostname
Locale
Timezone

before starting installation.

Example:

Shell
America/New_York

good

Shell
America/NewYork

rejected.

5. Confirmation Screen

Before wiping:

Plain Text
Hostname: archlinux
Timezone: America/New_York
Locale: en_US
Disk: /dev/nvme0n1
 
Proceed? [Y/N]

This prevents accidental installs.

Tier 2: Features Most Arch Users Expect
6. Microcode Installation

Detect CPU:

Shell
grep Intel /proc/cpuinfo

Install:

Plain Text
intel-ucode

or:

Plain Text
amd-ucode

automatically.

7. Install Common Packages

Many people immediately install:

Plain Text
git
base-devel
openssh
curl
wget

Provide a checkbox-style menu.

8. Desktop Environment Installation

Optional menu:

Plain Text
1. No desktop
2. KDE Plasma
3. GNOME
4. XFCE

This would dramatically increase usability.

9. SSH Server Option

Prompt:

Plain Text
Install and enable OpenSSH? [Y/N]

Especially useful for homelab deployments.

10. Automatic TRIM for SSDs

Enable:

Shell
systemctl enable fstrim.timer

Most SSD-based systems benefit from this.

Tier 3: Nice Enterprise/Homelab Features

Given your background, these might interest you the most.

11. Support for VirtIO Drives

Add support for:

Plain Text
/dev/vda

and you'll immediately work well in:

Proxmox
KVM
Many cloud VMs
12. Automated Host Naming

For example:

Plain Text
arch-desktop
arch-laptop
arch-server

Or generate from machine info.

13. Offline Package Cache

Useful when deploying multiple machines.

Install once:

Plain Text
archlinux cache

then install many systems from local media.

14. Configuration Profiles

Examples:

Plain Text
Desktop
Laptop
Server
Developer Workstation

Each installs a different package set.

15. Post-Install Script Hooks

Very useful.

Example:

Shell
post_install.sh

Users can drop custom commands into:

Plain Text
hooks/

and your installer runs them.

Tier 4: "People Will Actually Download This"
16. TUI Interface

Use:

Plain Text
whiptail

or

Plain Text
dialog

instead of raw prompts.

Example:

Plain Text
+-----------------------+
| Select Timezone |
+-----------------------+
| America/New_York |
| America/Chicago |
| America/Denver |
+-----------------------+

This is probably the single biggest usability improvement you could make.

17. Logging

Something like:

Shell
LOGFILE="/var/log/archinstall.log"
 
exec > >(tee -a "$LOGFILE")
exec 2>&1

When a user says:

Plain Text
The installer failed.

you can ask for the log.

18. Dry-Run Mode

Show what would happen:

Shell
./installer.sh --dry-run

without touching disks.

19. Automatic Internet Check

Before pacstrap:

Shell
ping -c 1 archlinux.org

or

Shell
curl archlinux.org

Fail early if networking isn't working.

20. Automatic Mirror Selection

Run:

Shell
reflector

to choose fast mirrors.

This can significantly improve install speed.

If I Were Building This

My next additions would be:

User account creation
VirtIO (/dev/vda) support
Logging
Internet connectivity verification
CPU microcode detection
whiptail/dialog UI
Desktop environment selection

Those seven improvements would make it feel less like a script and more like a lightweight Arch installer that people could actually deploy on physical machines, laptops, VMs, and homelab servers with minimal manual work afterward.
